{
  "// 0": "HOW TO USE THIS FILE (these lines starting with // are only notes: delete them or leave them, the bot ignores them)",
  "// 1": "1. Copy this file to locales/<code>.json, for example locales/de.json or locales/pt-br.json. The bot finds it by itself after a restart, nothing else has to be registered. Switch to it with /setlang <code>.",
  "// 2": "2. Translate only the VALUES (the text on the right of the colon). Never change the KEYS (the text on the left): the bot finds the texts by them.",
  "// 3": "3. The file must stay valid JSON: every text in double quotes (a double quote inside a text is written \\\"), a comma after every line except the last one, and \\n for a new line (keep the \\n where it is).",
  "// 4": "4. Keep {placeholders} such as {user} or {count} exactly as they are: the bot fills them in. You may move them to wherever your grammar needs them. A text without a {placeholder} must not get one.",
  "// 5": "5. Keep HTML tags such as <b>, </b>, <i>, <code>, </code> and <a href=\"...\"> together with their closing tags. The words between them are translated, the tags are not.",
  "// 6": "6. Do not translate commands (/ban, /warn ...), #HASHTAGS, @names or the words inside <code></code> that are commands or settings values you have to type (on, off, ban, mute ...): users still type them in English.",
  "// 7": "7. LANGUAGE SETTINGS (the first 7 lines below): meta.name is the name of your language written in that language (it is shown in the /setlang list), meta.flag is its flag emoji, meta.plural is \"one-other\" (1 is singular, everything else plural, like English) or \"one-few-many\" (1, then 2-4, then 5 and more, like Polish or Russian).",
  "// 8": "8. time.day, time.hour, time.minute, time.second and every text whose key ends with .noun hold the word in up to three forms separated by | : \"one|few|many\". A language with two forms writes \"day|days|days\".",
  "// 9": "9. The word.* texts are single words (ban, kick, mute ...) that are put into sentences, so translate each of them as the word that fits those sentences.",
  "// 10": "10. A text you do not translate (or delete from the file) is shown in English, so a language can be finished step by step.",
  "// 11": "11. Not in this file on purpose: the answers of the root commands and of the global-ban staff commands (always English), and everything sent to the log chats (always English). Their lines in /help are here and are translated.",
